# Ask the robot whether a task may start. Served by syncai_robot_state on the
# relative name `begin_task`, so it lands on <robot_id>/begin_task (2026-11).
#
# The backend calls it as the first activity of every task run, before any
# step -- above all for a run a Temporal Schedule started by itself, which no
# REST-side check ever sees. The answer is a decision, taken and applied in one
# place: an accepted task IS running from this call on (RobotStatus.RUNNING),
# so two runs cannot both be let through, and there is no sample of
# `robot_state` to read a second too late.
#
# An accepted task must be closed with EndTask, exactly once. A refused one
# must NOT be: a refusal changes nothing here, and in particular never latches
# ERROR -- a schedule that fires on an unconfirmed robot fails, and the robot
# is still merely unconfirmed.
#
# Idempotent per task_id: a second begin_task for the task already running is
# accepted again. A caller that lost the first answer and retries would
# otherwise read BUSY against itself and leave the robot RUNNING for good.

# The caller's own id for the run, unique among runs that can be open at once
# (the backend sends its Temporal workflow id). EndTask must carry the same.
string task_id
# True when the run has a step that drives on the map (MOVE / PATROL /
# CHARGE). An unconfirmed robot refuses those and still takes a task without
# one: standing up or speaking needs no position.
bool needs_localization
---
uint8 NONE            = 0
# needs_localization, and no operator has confirmed the position (or the
# confirmation was withdrawn by a map switch or a new guess).
uint8 NOT_INITIALIZED = 1
# ERROR is latched: EVERY task is refused, needs_localization or not, until
# SetRobotState releases it.
uint8 ERROR_LATCHED   = 2
# Another task_id is running.
uint8 BUSY            = 3

bool accepted
uint8 code
# One English sentence, written for an operator; on a refusal it is the
# reason the run's first step fails with.
string message
